<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallia Aquitania</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aquitania, Roman Aquitania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gallia</a> Aquitania was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in south-western Gaul, organised under Augustus. It originally covered the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquitani">Aquitani</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, but was extended north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, taking in many Celtic peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Burdigala</a>, modern Bordeaux, became its capital. In late antiquity it was divided, the south-west becoming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novempopulania">Novempopulana</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania.jpg" alt="Gallia Aquitania" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Aquitania_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 418 Honorius rewarded his federates under King Wallia (415–418) with land in the Garonne valley of <b>Gallia Aquitania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome their chief town was Turba, later Tarbes, within the province of <b>Aquitania</b> and then Novempopulana.</blockquote><a href="/state/bigerriones">Bigerriones</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome their territory became a civitas of <b>Aquitania</b>, the origin of the province of Berry.</blockquote><a href="/state/bituriges-cubi">Bituriges Cubi</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome Burdigala became the capital of <b>Aquitania</b> and a rich city whose vineyards, based on a vine named Biturica after the people, laid the foundations of the Bordeaux wine trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/bituriges-vivisci">Bituriges Vivisci</a></li><li><blockquote>Their land came under Roman control during the conquest of <b>Aquitania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/consoranni">Consoranni</a></li><li><blockquote>Their land was absorbed into <b>Roman Aquitania</b>; Caesar's legate Crassus campaigned in the region in 56 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/convenae">Convenae</a></li></ul></section>
